# G116 — Grayscale Converter

*v2. See `grayscale.md` for prose: row-major pixel buffer, closed-enum
conversion algorithms, Rec.709 luminosity coefficients, histogram over grayscale.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Thumbnail Pipeline

[thumbnail-pipeline @image-dir @algorithm
    IN ORDER TO
        convert every image in @image-dir to grayscale
        using the chosen algorithm
        and save thumbnails with matching contrast metrics recorded

    @images <- ![vault[find {path: @image-dir + "/*.png"}]]

    for @image-path in @images
        @img  <- [img[load {path: @image-path}]]
        @gray <- [img[convert {image: @img, algorithm: @algorithm}]]
        @hist <- [img[histogram    {gray: @gray}]]
        @mean <- [img[mean-brightness {gray: @gray}]]
        @sd   <- [img[contrast-stddev {gray: @gray}]]
        [vault[save {path: @image-path + ".thumb.gray",
                      image: @gray,
                      metadata: {mean: @mean, stddev: @sd}}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[img
    new          {width, height}
    filled       {width, height, color}
    from-pixels  {width, height, pixels}      ;; || resistance if mismatched
    get          {image, x, y}                ;; || nil if out of bounds
    set          {image, x, y, pixel}         ;; || nil if out of bounds
    convert      {image, algorithm}           ;; algorithm: "Luminosity" | "Average" | "Lightness" | "Red" | "Green" | "Blue"
    histogram    {gray}
    mean-brightness {gray}
    contrast-stddev {gray}
    dynamic-range   {gray}                    ;; || nil if empty
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: algorithm as bare-word string

The v1 modeled the algorithm choice as a closed enum. In Innate, it's
a bare-word string the resolver interprets:

[img[convert {image: @photo, algorithm: "Luminosity"}]
    where
        "Luminosity" means 0.2126 R + 0.7152 G + 0.0722 B (Rec.709)
        and "Average"   means (R + G + B) / 3
        and "Lightness" means (max(R,G,B) + min(R,G,B)) / 2
        and "Red" | "Green" | "Blue" means that channel alone
]

The `where` clauses above double as human-readable documentation *and*
machine-parseable constraints the resolver validates the algorithm
string against. An unknown algorithm resists with "no such conversion."

---

## Out-of-bounds access as resistance

[img[get {image: @img, x: 9999, y: 9999}]
    || -> nil
]

The resolver emits resistance on out-of-bounds; `|| -> nil` falls back
to nil for callers who'd rather sample than crash.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @red <- [img[filled {width: 4, height: 4,
                          color: {r: 255, g: 0, b: 0}}]]

    @gray-lum  <- [img[convert {image: @red, algorithm: "Luminosity"}]]
    -> [img[get {gray: @gray-lum, x: 0, y: 0}]]     ;; -> 54  (Rec.709 red)

    @gray-avg  <- [img[convert {image: @red, algorithm: "Average"}]]
    -> [img[get {gray: @gray-avg, x: 0, y: 0}]]      ;; -> 85

    @hist <- [img[histogram {gray: @gray-lum}]]
    -> @hist[54]                                      ;; -> 16 (all 16 pixels are 54)
]
